{"method":"judgment","probability":0.25,"rationale":"I estimate a 25% chance of a qualifying Neutron launch before the contract deadline of January 1, 2027, at 04:59 UTC. The supplied August 10 company statement reported ongoing first-flight hardware assembly, integration, and testing, with stage 1 tank production aligned to vehicle delivery to the launch pad in Q4 2026. That is evidence of progress, but pad delivery is not launch readiness or launch itself. The packet does not establish a completed integrated-test sequence or a firm launch date.

With roughly three and a half months remaining at the information cutoff, a launch is plausible if pad delivery occurs early in Q4 and subsequent preparations proceed smoothly. My general-background assumption is that inaugural rocket campaigns have substantial schedule risk: integrated testing and launch-site operations can expose issues requiring rework. This supports a probability materially below 50%, rather than treating the company's pad-delivery target as a launch commitment.

The strongest reason this estimate could be too high is that even on-time Q4 pad delivery could leave insufficient time for testing and corrective work. The strongest reason it could be too low is that substantial progress may have occurred after August 10, enabling early delivery and a short launch campaign. I assess the stated launch event, not successful orbital insertion.","limitations":["The company schedule is forward-looking and not independently verified readiness evidence.","The small supplied packet does not establish progress after August 10 or the current status of all launch-critical systems.","The probability is a judgment informed by general first-flight schedule risk, not a statistically calibrated estimate.","Only the supplied definition is available; additional frozen settlement rules could clarify what qualifies as a launch."],"evidence_refs":[{"packet_id":"pkt_f076eb65826cb92984699c5b","record_id":"neutron_q2"}]}

